WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
The median.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.353
When someone says average, ask which one.

3
00:00:04.353 --> 00:00:06.567
For skewed data it changes the story.

4
00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:11.133
The median is the middle value once the data is sorted.

5
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:13.956
Half the points sit below it, half above.

6
00:00:13.956 --> 00:00:17.033
It's the fiftieth percentile, also called p fifty.

7
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:22.033
It only cares about position in the sorted order, never how large the extremes are.

8
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:24.437
Here's how it works.

9
00:00:24.437 --> 00:00:25.400
Sort the values.

10
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.200
With five values, the median is simply the middle one: four.

11
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.275
Now add an outlier of forty.

12
00:00:31.275 --> 00:00:35.521
The middle pair is four and five, so the median is four point five.

13
00:00:35.521 --> 00:00:38.500
It barely moved, while the mean shot up to ten.

14
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.667
Let's compare the two side by side.

15
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:44.427
The median depends on sorted position.

16
00:00:44.427 --> 00:00:46.433
The mean lets every value pull.

17
00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:50.700
The median minimizes absolute distance, the mean squared distance.

18
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.570
So the median is robust to outliers and the mean is not.

19
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.567
For skewed data, that choice changes the story.

20
00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.133
Here it is in NumPy.

21
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:03.633
Ten rocky planet masses, and then we add one Jupiter.

22
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:08.299
The median goes from zero point nine seven five to exactly one.

23
00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:09.200
Barely moved.

24
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.000
The mean, though, explodes to nearly thirty.

25
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.100
Now back to the latency dashboard.

26
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.879
Switch to median and p ninety five.

27
00:01:17.879 --> 00:01:22.200
The p fifty is point nine seconds, so typical users are fast.

28
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.338
The p ninety five is two point one seconds.

29
00:01:25.338 --> 00:01:29.033
That slow tail, the reranker cold start, is what to fix.

30
00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:32.500
The same idea applies to your evals.

31
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.333
Say one answer in your RAGAS run is catastrophically bad.

32
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:40.500
The median faithfulness score barely notices, but the mean sinks.

33
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.667
Check both, and the gap between them tells you more than either number alone.

34
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:46.632
Let's recap.

35
00:01:46.632 --> 00:01:49.567
The median is the middle value of sorted data.

36
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.567
It's robust to outliers, where the mean is not.

37
00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:57.644
And the same idea generalizes to p ninety five and p ninety nine.

38
00:01:57.644 --> 00:01:58.967
Next up: Variance.
